      "message": {
        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This study provides clinical outcome data for inosine supplementation combined with immune-checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in 172 patients with advanced malignant solid tumors. The authors reported that the inosine group had a significantly prolonged median progression-free survival (PFS) compared to the control group (7.00 vs 4.40 months; HR 0.63, p = 0.011) and a higher objective response rate (ORR) trend (26.7% vs 15.1%; p = 0.061). The study also identified blood-based biomarkers (e.g., PLR <229, NLR <4, LDH ≥216) associated with greater PFS benefit in the inosine group. Safety data indicated no increase in Grade 3-4 adverse reactions with inosine supplementation (29% vs 36%).\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study design was a single-center, prospective, randomized, open-label Phase 2 trial (NCT05809336) conducted from January 2021 to December 2022. The inosine dosage was 0.2 g three times/day. The discussion section references preclinical mechanisms (e.g., UBA6 inhibition, A2A receptor interaction, T-cell activation) from other studies to explain the observed clinical effects, though this trial did not perform direct molecular mechanism validation. Subgroup analyses showed stronger PFS signals in lung cancer and digestive system malignancies.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific success criterion requiring absolute quantitative concentrations (μM or ng/mL) of inosine measured directly in human tumor tissue samples is not addressed by this material. The study measured clinical efficacy endpoints (PFS, OS, ORR) and blood-based inflammatory markers but did not report metabolomic profiling or inosine concentration measurements within tumor biopsies. Consequently, no correlation between tissue inosine levels and ICI efficacy endpoints can be derived from this text.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Use this study to support the clinical rationale and safety profile of inosine as an ICI combination strategy in the review. Cite the PFS and ORR results as evidence of clinical benefit. However, explicitly note that tissue pharmacokinetic data is lacking. Continue searching for studies employing tumor tissue metabolomics or biopsy-based quantification to fulfill the specific concentration-correlation gap.\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze a captured paper text against a specific literature-planning gap.\n\n**Gap Question:** \"What are the absolute quantitative concentrations (μM or ng/mL) of inosine measured directly in human solid tumor tissue samples from ICI-treated patients, and how do these correlate with response outcomes?\"\n\n**Success Criteria:** \"Identification of at least one study reporting absolute inosine concentration values in human tumor tissue (not just plasma stratification) with statistical correlation to ICI efficacy endpoints (ORR, PFS, or OS).\"\n\n**Material:** A full-text article describing a single-center, prospective, randomized, open-label Phase 2 study (NCT05809336) conducted in Beijing. It compares an inosine supplementation group (0.2g, 3x/day) + PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitor vs. PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitor alone in 172 patients with advanced malignant solid tumors.\n\n**Task:**\n1.  Describe what the study itself studied and found.\n2.  Explain how that material may help with the requested gap.\n3.  Assign status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable).\n4.  Assign material_usefulness (direct, supporting, none).\n5.  Populate useful_material, auxiliary_material, remaining_gap, outline_action.\n6.  Adhere to constraints: Do not call the gap fulfilled if success criteria remain open. Do not infer study design/results from silence. Keep findings attached to the study.\n\n**Analysis of the Gap vs. Material:**\n*   **Gap Requirement:** Absolute quantitative concentrations of inosine *measured directly in human solid tumor tissue samples* from ICI-treated patients, correlated with response.\n*   **Study Content:** This is an interventional trial where patients *received* oral inosine supplementation. It measures clinical outcomes (ORR, PFS, OS).\n*   **Measurement:** The text describes efficacy assessments (CT/MRI, tumor markers) and safety (adverse events). It mentions subgroup analyses based on blood markers (PLR, NLR, LDH, ENR, CRP).\n*   **Tissue Concentration:** I need to check if the text reports measuring inosine levels *in the tumor tissue*.\n    *   Scanning the \"Methods\" section: \"Antitumor efficacy was evaluated... using computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging and tumor markers.\" No mention of tissue biopsy for inosine quantification.\n    *   Scanning the \"Results\" section: Reports ORR, PFS, OS, adverse events, subgroup analyses based on blood ratios (PLR, NLR, etc.). No mention of tissue inosine concentrations (μM or ng/mL).\n    *   Scanning the \"Background/Discussion\": Mentions preclinical studies (Mager et al., Wang et al., Zhang et al.) where mechanisms were explored (e.g., TILs, IFN-gamma), but this specific clinical study does not report tissue concentration data.\n    *   Conclusion: This study tests the *effect of supplementation* on outcomes, not the *correlation of endogenous or exogenous tissue concentration* with outcomes. It does not measure inosine levels in the tumor tissue.\n\n**Status Determination:**\n*   The success criteria require \"absolute inosine concentration values in human tumor tissue\".\n*   This study does *not* provide these values.\n*   Therefore, the gap status regarding *this specific material* is **unmet**.\n*   However, the material is **supporting** because it establishes clinical efficacy of inosine supplementation with ICI, which is contextually relevant to the topic (inosine + ICI), even if it doesn't answer the specific pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic (PK/PD) tissue concentration question. It shows *that* inosine matters clinically, even if it doesn't show *how much* is in the tissue.\n\n**Drafting the Response:**\n*   **Status:** unmet (The specific success criterion of tissue concentration measurement is not met).\n*   **Material Usefulness:** supporting (It provides clinical outcome data for inosine + ICI, which is relevant context, but does not answer the specific quantification gap).\n*   **Useful Material:** Clinical efficacy results (PFS, ORR), safety data, subgroup analyses (blood markers). This supports the general hypothesis that inosine affects ICI outcomes.\n*   **Auxiliary Material:** Mechanism discussion (citing other papers), safety profile, dosing regimen.\n*   **Remaining Gap:** The specific need for tissue concentration data (μM/ng/mL) correlated with outcomes is still open. This study only measures clinical response to supplementation, not tissue levels.\n*   **Outline Action:** Note that this study confirms clinical benefit but lacks PK/PD tissue data. Future search should focus on studies with metabolomic profiling of tumor biopsies.\n\n**Constraints Check:**\n*   Do not call the gap fulfilled. (Status = unmet).\n*   Refer to paper as 'this study' or 'the authors'.\n*   Do not infer design from silence. (Explicitly state tissue concentration was not reported).\n*   Keep findings attached to the study.\n*   JSON format.\n\n**Refining the \"Status\":**\nThe instruction says: \"Treat the captured text as the main material... Explain any direct contribution, then preserve useful supporting material... Keep the primary gap judgment separate from material usefulness: use status fulfilled, partial, unmet, or unjudgeable\".\nSince the success criteria are specific (\"Identification of at least one study reporting absolute inosine concentration values in human tumor tissue...\"), and this study does *not* report that, the status for *this gap* based on *this material* is **unmet**.\nHowever, the material itself is useful for the broader review (as indicated in the review_planning_B). So material_usefulness = supporting.\n\n**Wait, one nuance:** The gap asks for \"inosine measured directly in human solid tumor tissue samples from ICI-treated patients\". This study treats patients with inosine + ICI. Did they measure tissue levels? The text says \"Antitumor efficacy was evaluated... using computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging and tumor markers.\" It does not mention metabolomics or tissue biopsy analysis for inosine levels. So, definitely unmet on the concentration metric.\n\n**Structure:**\n1.  **Status:** unmet\n2.  **Material_usefulness:** supporting\n3.  **Useful_material:** Summary of clinical outcomes (PFS, ORR) showing inosine supplementation improves ICI efficacy. Safety data. Blood-based biomarker subgroups.\n4.  **Auxiliary_material:** Dosing info, mechanism discussion (citing others), study design details.\n5.  **Remaining_gap:** Lack of tissue concentration data.\n6.  **Outline_action:** Use this for clinical efficacy context, but continue searching for metabolomic studies.\n\n**Writing the content:**\n*   Start with what the study studied: Prospective randomized Phase 2 trial, inosine supplementation + ICI vs ICI alone in 172 patients with solid tumors.\n*   Findings: Improved PFS (7.0 vs 4.4 months), trend in ORR (26.7% vs 15.1%), safe.\n*   Gap relation: Does not measure tissue inosine concentrations. Only measures clinical outcomes.\n*   Usefulness: Supports the biological plausibility and clinical relevance of inosine in ICI therapy, but does not provide the specific PK/PD tissue correlation requested.\n\n**JSON Construction:**\nEnsure valid JSON. No markdown outside the JSON block? The prompt says \"Return one JSON object\". I should output *only* the JSON.\n\nWait, looking at the instruction: \"Return one JSON object with status, material_usefulness, useful_material, auxiliary_material, remaining_gap, and outline_action.\"\nI need to make sure the text inside the JSON is escaped properly.\n\n**Double Check Success Criteria:**\n\"Identification of at least one study reporting absolute inosine concentration values in human tumor tissue... with statistical correlation to ICI efficacy endpoints\".\nThis study reports efficacy endpoints (PFS, ORR) but *not* the concentration values in tissue. So the criteria are not met by this paper.\nStatus: unmet.\n\n**Material Usefulness:**\nIt is \"supporting\" because